WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.301
Two shapes of eval.

2
00:00:01.581 --> 00:00:04.333
Evals come in two broad shapes, and you need both.

3
00:00:04.613 --> 00:00:10.437
Unit-test-style evals are assertions: specific, fast, cheap checks on specific inputs.

4
00:00:10.717 --> 00:00:12.083
“The output parses.

5
00:00:12.363 --> 00:00:14.901
” “Every evidence quote appears in the transcript.

6
00:00:15.181 --> 00:00:19.149
” “When the user asks to delete their data, the reply offers a handoff.

7
00:00:19.429 --> 00:00:27.451
” Husain calls these level 1 in his essay and recommends running them on every code change, organized by feature and scenario.

8
00:00:27.731 --> 00:00:40.189
One difference from software unit tests: the target pass rate is a product decision, not automatically 100 percent, because some checks cover behavior the model gets right most but not all of the time.

9
00:00:40.469 --> 00:00:52.992
Benchmark-style evals are scored sets: a few hundred representative cases, a grader, and an aggregate number (accuracy, pass rate, a failure-mode rate) you compare across versions and models.

10
00:00:53.272 --> 00:00:56.429
They answer “is version B better than version A overall?

11
00:00:56.709 --> 00:00:58.523
” and “which model should we use?

12
00:00:58.803 --> 00:00:59.100
”.

13
00:00:59.380 --> 00:01:01.812
Anthropic’s post adds a second axis.

14
00:01:02.092 --> 00:01:09.474
Capability evals target things the system can’t yet do well, so they start with low pass rates and give you a hill to climb.

15
00:01:09.754 --> 00:01:14.767
Regression evals protect things that already work and should stay near 100 percent.

16
00:01:15.047 --> 00:01:20.764
When a capability eval’s pass rate gets high, its cases “graduate” into the regression suite.

17
00:01:21.044 --> 00:01:30.858
An eval that every version passes (a saturated eval) still guards against regressions but no longer tells you how to improve, so you add harder cases.

18
00:01:31.138 --> 00:01:32.802
In practice you start small.

19
00:01:33.082 --> 00:01:44.964
The same post suggests 20 to 50 simple tasks drawn from real failures as a good beginning, and warns that waiting for a perfect set of hundreds only makes evals harder to start.
